// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
//
// Expected: f0f1f0 — a bundle is fresh if ANY field is, but a field projected
// out of it is fresh only if that field is. Bob splits one bundle into a
// long-term identifier and a nonce, and the two halves have opposite verdicts
// even though they came out of the same term.
//
// This is the model that would catch freshness being read off the container
// rather than off the value. got_id resolves through the SPLIT to the
// identifier alone, and answering that query from the bundle it was projected
// from would call a replayable identifier fresh — which is the whole failure
// mode freshness queries exist to detect.

//
// The wire values are guarded, and that is not decoration. Under an active
// attacker an unguarded value is one the attacker may replace, so the
// recipient's state resolves it to the bare constant rather than to whatever
// the sender computed — and a bare constant transitively contains nothing at
// all, fresh or otherwise. Ungarding any message here turns every verdict
// below into f1 for a reason that has nothing to do with what this model is
// about. freshness_unguarded_wire.vp is where that effect is the subject.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private device_id
	generates nonce
	bundle = CONCAT(device_id, nonce)
]

Alice -> Bob: [bundle]

principal Bob[
	got_id, got_nonce = SPLIT(bundle)
	_ = HASH(got_id, got_nonce)
]

queries[
	freshness? bundle
	freshness? got_id
	freshness? got_nonce
]
